          "claim": "By induction along [L3] on $n \\in \\omega$, the statement \"$T(k) \\in T(k+1)$ for every $k \\le n$, and $T(n+1) = \\aleph_{T(n)}$\" holds for every $n$. At $n = 0$: $\\operatorname{ran}(T \\restriction 1) = \\{T(0)\\}$ by [L4], so $T(1) = \\aleph_{T(0)} = \\aleph_{\\aleph_0}$, and $0 \\in \\aleph_0$ gives $\\aleph_0 \\in \\aleph_{\\aleph_0}$ by the strict increase in [L1], that is $T(0) \\in T(1)$. At $n+1$: the statement at $n$ makes $T(0) \\subseteq \\cdots \\subseteq T(n+1)$ by [L5], so $\\bigcup \\operatorname{ran}(T \\restriction (n+2)) = T(n+1)$ and $T(n+2) = \\aleph_{T(n+1)}$; and $T(n) \\in T(n+1)$ together with strict increase gives $\\aleph_{T(n)} \\in \\aleph_{T(n+1)}$, that is $T(n+1) \\in T(n+2)$. [step 1.1, L1, L3, L4, L5]",
