43:The claim is plausible because the **weak** form is true —
55:[L1] $\kappa \le \lambda$ implies $\kappa^{\mu} \le \lambda^{\mu}$; and $(\mu^{\nu})^{\rho} = \mu^{\nu \otimes \rho}$ ([[lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws]]).
57:[L2] $\kappa \otimes \kappa = \kappa$ for every infinite cardinal $\kappa$ ([[thm-hessenberg]]).
59:[L3] $\kappa < 2^{\kappa}$ and $2^{\kappa}$ is a cardinal ([[thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor]]).
61:[L4] $\aleph_1 = \aleph_0^{+}$ is the least cardinal strictly above $\aleph_0$, and $\aleph_0 < \aleph_1$ ([[def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies]], [[lem-successor-cardinal-exists]], [[cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined]]).
63:[L5] $\aleph_0 = \omega$ is an infinite cardinal and $2 \in \omega$ is a cardinal with $2 \le \aleph_0$ ([[thm-cardinal-arithmetic-agrees-with-finite-counting]], [[def-cardinal]], [[def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies]]).
65:[L6] Ordinals satisfy trichotomy and $\alpha \notin \alpha$ ([[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]], [[lem-ordinal-basics]]).
71:1.1 Suppose, for contradiction, that $\kappa < \lambda$ implies $\kappa^{\mu} < \lambda^{\mu}$ for all cardinals. [assume-contra]
73:1.2 By [L3] the cardinal $2^{\aleph_0}$ satisfies $\aleph_0 < 2^{\aleph_0}$, so [L4] gives $\aleph_1 \le 2^{\aleph_0}$; with [L5] this chains to $2 \le \aleph_0 < \aleph_1 \le 2^{\aleph_0}$. [L3, L4, L5]
75:2.1 Applying [L1] along that chain, $2^{\aleph_0} \le \aleph_0^{\aleph_0} \le \aleph_1^{\aleph_0} \le (2^{\aleph_0})^{\aleph_0} = 2^{\aleph_0 \otimes \aleph_0} = 2^{\aleph_0}$, the last two equalities by [L1] and [L2]; so all four values are equal and in particular $\aleph_0^{\aleph_0} = \aleph_1^{\aleph_0}$. [step 1.2, L1, L2, L5, L6]
77:3.1 But $\aleph_0 < \aleph_1$ by step 1.2, so the assumed claim at $\kappa = \aleph_0$, $\lambda = \aleph_1$, $\mu = \aleph_0$ gives $\aleph_0^{\aleph_0} < \aleph_1^{\aleph_0}$, contradicting step 2.1 by [L6]; therefore exponentiation is not strictly monotone in the base. [step 1.1, step 1.2, step 2.1, L6, discharge-contradiction] ∎
83:**The weak form is not damaged.** $\kappa \le \lambda \Rightarrow \kappa^{\mu} \le \lambda^{\mu}$ remains true, and so does strictness in the exponent slot in the one case proved on this page, $\kappa < 2^{\kappa}$. What fails is the strict form in the base, and it fails at the smallest infinite instance.

